1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:02,240
Right now, you are moving.

2
00:00:02,640 --> 00:00:05,740
If you are in Britain, the
ground under you is carrying

3
00:00:05,740 --> 00:00:09,220
you east at about a thousand
kilometres an hour.

4
00:00:09,760 --> 00:00:13,240
At the equator it is faster
than a jet airliner.

5
00:00:13,800 --> 00:00:16,420
And you feel nothing at all.

6
00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:20,020
So what would happen if
Earth stopped spinning?

7
00:00:20,280 --> 00:00:21,680
Not slowly.

8
00:00:21,740 --> 00:00:23,140
All at once.

9
00:00:23,260 --> 00:00:24,880
Now imagine the impossible.

10
00:00:25,080 --> 00:00:28,040
Earth's rock stops turning in an instant.

11
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:29,780
But the air does not.

12
00:00:30,220 --> 00:00:32,020
Nothing has told it to stop.

13
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:35,100
At the equator the atmosphere
would keep going east at

14
00:00:35,100 --> 00:00:38,080
more than one and a half
thousand kilometres an hour.

15
00:00:38,360 --> 00:00:40,520
That is faster than the speed of sound,

16
00:00:40,520 --> 00:00:44,580
and several times faster than the
strongest gust of wind ever measured.

17
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:49,840
The oceans would keep moving too, and
rise up against every eastern coast.

18
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:51,380
Why east?

19
00:00:51,380 --> 00:00:54,260
Because that is the way we
were all already going.

20
00:00:54,460 --> 00:00:57,860
Anything not fixed to the ground
would carry on east as well,

21
00:00:57,900 --> 00:01:01,040
just as you lurch forwards
when a bus brakes hard.

22
00:01:01,620 --> 00:01:06,020
Here in Britain the wind would arrive
at about a thousand kilometres an hour.

23
00:01:06,580 --> 00:01:08,500
But here is a strange detail.

24
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:12,800
Stand exactly at the North Pole,
and you would hardly notice.

25
00:01:12,880 --> 00:01:15,160
You were barely moving in the first place.

26
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:18,060
You were just turning on
the spot, once a day.

27
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:19,940
And it cannot happen.

28
00:01:19,980 --> 00:01:21,500
There is no brake.

29
00:01:21,700 --> 00:01:25,120
The energy stored in Earth's
spin is as much as all the

30
00:01:25,120 --> 00:01:29,140
sunlight that reaches Earth in
about forty thousand years.

31
00:01:29,520 --> 00:01:33,200
Nothing in our solar system
could take that away in a moment.

32
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:34,940
Why don't you feel it?

33
00:01:34,940 --> 00:01:36,720
Because the speed is steady.

34
00:01:36,840 --> 00:01:40,820
Sit in a smooth airliner and you can
pour a drink without spilling a drop.

35
00:01:40,920 --> 00:01:44,240
Nothing pushes you, because
nothing is changing.

36
00:01:44,440 --> 00:01:48,800
Your body only notices a change
in speed, never the speed itself.

37
00:01:48,960 --> 00:01:51,980
You can still see the
spin, every single day.

38
00:01:51,980 --> 00:01:53,720
The Sun does not rise.

39
00:01:53,720 --> 00:01:56,220
Your side of Earth turns towards it.

40
00:01:56,440 --> 00:02:02,580
Earth turns from west to east, and that is
why the Sun always comes up in the east,

41
00:02:02,580 --> 00:02:05,720
and why the stars wheel
across the sky at night.

42
00:02:06,100 --> 00:02:08,080
The spin does leave clues.

43
00:02:08,220 --> 00:02:11,540
Because the ground at the
equator swings round so fast,

44
00:02:11,540 --> 00:02:15,580
you weigh about half of one percent
less there than at the poles.

45
00:02:15,680 --> 00:02:17,800
And the whole planet bulges.

46
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,460
Earth is about forty-three
kilometres wider

47
00:02:20,460 --> 00:02:23,760
across the equator than
it is from pole to pole.

48
00:02:23,980 --> 00:02:28,540
In 1851, in Paris, Léon
Foucault hung a heavy ball

49
00:02:28,540 --> 00:02:32,740
from a wire sixty-seven metres
long and set it swinging.

50
00:02:32,960 --> 00:02:36,800
Over the hours, the line of
its swing slowly turned.

51
00:02:36,920 --> 00:02:38,800
The pendulum was not turning.

52
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:40,200
The floor was.

53
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:44,600
Anyone could stand there and watch
Earth spin without looking at the sky.

54
00:02:44,900 --> 00:02:47,040
We make one of these every week.

55
00:02:47,120 --> 00:02:49,820
Subscribing is how the next one finds you.

56
00:02:50,320 --> 00:02:53,120
So a sudden stop is a thought experiment.

57
00:02:53,340 --> 00:02:54,900
What about a slow one?

58
00:02:55,320 --> 00:02:58,220
Suppose Earth slowed down gently,

59
00:02:58,220 --> 00:03:02,020
over thousands of years,
until it did not turn at all.

60
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:05,040
Remember that bulge at the equator?

61
00:03:05,560 --> 00:03:08,280
The spin is what holds
the oceans out there.

62
00:03:08,680 --> 00:03:12,440
Without it, the water would
drain away towards the poles.

63
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:14,660
Mapmakers have modelled it.

64
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:18,480
The seas gather into
two great polar oceans,

65
00:03:18,560 --> 00:03:23,600
and a single enormous band of dry land
wraps around the middle of the world.

66
00:03:24,440 --> 00:03:26,440
And the day itself would change.

67
00:03:26,680 --> 00:03:32,300
With no spin at all, the Sun would cross
the sky just once as Earth went round it.

68
00:03:32,860 --> 00:03:39,120
One day would last a whole year: six months
of daylight, then six months of night.

69
00:03:39,780 --> 00:03:43,260
The day side would bake
under a Sun that never sets,

70
00:03:43,360 --> 00:03:46,140
and the night side would
freeze in the dark,

71
00:03:46,160 --> 00:03:49,940
with the wind always pouring
from one side to the other.

72
00:03:51,240 --> 00:03:53,360
We have a neighbour that comes close.

73
00:03:53,560 --> 00:03:58,240
Venus turns once every two hundred
and forty-three Earth days,

74
00:03:58,400 --> 00:04:00,020
and it turns backwards.

75
00:04:00,400 --> 00:04:02,719
Its day is longer than its year.

76
00:04:03,460 --> 00:04:07,920
If you saw our Venus film, you know
how strange that world became.

77
00:04:08,380 --> 00:04:09,860
Here is the twist.

78
00:04:10,100 --> 00:04:13,040
Earth is slowing down already, right now.

79
00:04:13,480 --> 00:04:17,400
The Moon pulls on our oceans
and raises the tides.

80
00:04:17,580 --> 00:04:23,260
As Earth turns beneath those tides, the
water drags a little against the seabed,

81
00:04:23,260 --> 00:04:26,040
like a hand resting on a spinning wheel.

82
00:04:26,600 --> 00:04:30,080
Every day, a tiny amount of spin is lost.

83
00:04:30,460 --> 00:04:31,860
Where does it go?

84
00:04:32,020 --> 00:04:33,420
Into the Moon.

85
00:04:33,580 --> 00:04:36,220
The tides tug the Moon forwards,

86
00:04:36,260 --> 00:04:40,680
and it drifts about four centimetres
further from us every year.

87
00:04:41,360 --> 00:04:45,660
If you saw our Moon film, this
is the other half of that story.

88
00:04:46,040 --> 00:04:49,300
And the Moon shows us
where this road leads.

89
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:53,780
Long ago, Earth's pull
slowed the Moon's own

90
00:04:53,780 --> 00:04:58,000
spin until one turn
took exactly one orbit.

91
00:04:58,360 --> 00:05:02,020
That is why the Moon always
shows us the same face.

92
00:05:02,260 --> 00:05:03,940
It did not stop spinning.

93
00:05:03,940 --> 00:05:05,340
It got locked.

94
00:05:05,760 --> 00:05:07,160
How do we know?

95
00:05:07,560 --> 00:05:09,220
Ancient eclipses.

96
00:05:09,520 --> 00:05:11,260
Astronomers in Babylon,

97
00:05:11,260 --> 00:05:16,880
China and medieval Europe wrote down
where and when the Sun went dark.

98
00:05:17,140 --> 00:05:19,840
If Earth had kept
spinning at today's rate,

99
00:05:19,880 --> 00:05:21,820
those eclipses would have been seen from

100
00:05:21,820 --> 00:05:25,420
places hundreds or thousands
of kilometres away.

101
00:05:25,860 --> 00:05:29,760
The records only fit if the
day has been growing longer,

102
00:05:30,060 --> 00:05:34,100
by nearly two thousandths
of a second every century.

103
00:05:34,640 --> 00:05:36,160
That sounds like nothing.

104
00:05:36,360 --> 00:05:37,760
But give it time.

105
00:05:38,020 --> 00:05:43,700
Corals lay down a fine growth line
every day, and a thicker band each year.

106
00:05:44,100 --> 00:05:47,260
Fossil corals about three
hundred and eighty million

107
00:05:47,260 --> 00:05:51,700
years old show about four
hundred daily lines in a year.

108
00:05:52,180 --> 00:05:56,300
Back then, a day was only
about twenty-two hours long.

109
00:05:56,780 --> 00:05:59,640
So who notices two
thousandths of a second?

110
00:05:59,640 --> 00:06:01,040
Your phone does.

111
00:06:01,620 --> 00:06:05,480
Since the late 1960s the second
has been set by atomic clocks,

112
00:06:05,480 --> 00:06:07,900
which do not care how fast Earth turns.

113
00:06:07,900 --> 00:06:12,020
But the day, measured by the Sun,
does not quite fit the clocks.

114
00:06:12,100 --> 00:06:15,920
So since 1972, timekeepers have
added an extra second to the

115
00:06:15,920 --> 00:06:20,740
world's clocks twenty-seven times,
to keep noon where it belongs.

116
00:06:21,060 --> 00:06:23,860
The last one came at the end of 2016.

117
00:06:23,900 --> 00:06:25,620
And the day wobbles.

118
00:06:25,940 --> 00:06:31,500
Earth's molten core, its oceans and
even melting ice all nudge the spin.

119
00:06:31,740 --> 00:06:35,620
In the last few years the planet has
actually been running a touch fast,

120
00:06:35,620 --> 00:06:38,940
and some days have come in
more than a millisecond short.

121
00:06:39,360 --> 00:06:43,920
Timekeepers now plan to stop
adding leap seconds by 2035.

122
00:06:44,180 --> 00:06:46,340
So Earth will never stop spinning.

123
00:06:46,440 --> 00:06:49,440
Even the Moon's slow
brake would need tens of

124
00:06:49,440 --> 00:06:52,860
billions of years to lock
our day to its month,

125
00:06:52,900 --> 00:06:56,880
and long before then the Sun will
have changed beyond recognition.

126
00:06:57,060 --> 00:07:00,720
Your days will stay twenty-four
hours, to within a blink.

127
00:07:01,600 --> 00:07:05,740
What if the most surprising thing about
your day is not that it might end,

128
00:07:05,740 --> 00:07:08,040
but that it has always been changing?

129
00:07:08,280 --> 00:07:11,440
The bigger question is the
one underneath all of this.

130
00:07:11,780 --> 00:07:14,500
Something set Earth spinning
in the first place,

131
00:07:14,540 --> 00:07:17,240
about four and a half billion years ago.

132
00:07:17,700 --> 00:07:26,200
Every sunrise you have ever seen is
that first push, still running down.

